      "text": "To find \\(\\sin \\frac{11 \\pi}{3}\\), we can use the periodicity of the sine function. The sine function has a period of \\(2\\pi\\), which means \\(\\sin x = \\sin(x + 2k\\pi)\\) for any integer \\(k\\).\n\nFirst, we reduce \\(\\frac{11 \\pi}{3}\\) modulo \\(2\\pi\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11 \\pi}{3} = 3 \\pi + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(3 \\pi\\) is a multiple of the period \\(2\\pi\\), it can be removed from the sine function without changing its value:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{11 \\pi}{3} = \\sin \\left(3 \\pi + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we find \\(\\sin \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\). The angle \\(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\) is in the second quadrant, and its reference angle is \\(\\pi - \\frac{2 \\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\). The sine of an angle in the second quadrant is positive, so:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{2 \\pi}{3} = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\sin \\frac{11 \\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
