      "text": "To find \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\), we need to reduce the angle \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) to an equivalent angle within the standard range of \\(0\\) to \\(2\\pi\\). This can be done by subtracting \\(2\\pi\\) (which is \\(\\frac{6\\pi}{3}\\)) from \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) until the result is in the standard range.\n\nLet's perform the calculation step-by-step:\n\n1. Start with the angle \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\).\n2. Subtract \\(2\\pi\\) (which is \\(\\frac{6\\pi}{3}\\)) from \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = \\frac{11\\pi}{3} - \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is equivalent to \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) in the standard range of angles.\n\n3. Now, we need to find \\(\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\). The angle \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is in the fourth quadrant, where the sine function is negative.\n\n4. The reference angle for \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n2\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{6\\pi}{3} - \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nThe sine of \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) is \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). Since \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is in the fourth quadrant, the sine value is negative:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = -\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]\n\n$ \\triangle ABC$ has $ 2$, $ 3$, and $ 4$ sided polygon $ J$ as its incenter. If the sum of $ AB$, $ BC$, and $ CA$ equals $ X$ units, what is the value of $ JAJB \\plus{} JBJC \\plus{} JCJC$ in units?\n(Notice that $ J$ is on side $ BC$ of $ \\triangle ABC$ and $ AJ \\perp BC$, etc.) Given a triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\) with incenter \\( J \\), we know that \\( J \\) is the incenter of the triangle, which means it is the intersection point of the angle bisectors. The incenter always divides each angle bisector into segments that are proportional to the adjacent sides. Specifically, in \\( \\triangle ABC \\), \\( JAJB