      "text": "To find \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\), we need to reduce the angle \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) to an equivalent angle within the interval \\([0, 2\\pi)\\). This can be done by subtracting multiples of \\(2\\pi\\) from \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nFirst, let's find how many full rotations of \\(2\\pi\\) fit into \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} \\div 2\\pi = \\frac{11\\pi}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2\\pi} = \\frac{11}{6}\n\\]\n\nThis calculation tells us that \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is equivalent to \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi \\times \\frac{11}{6} = \\frac{11\\pi}{3} - \\frac{22\\pi}{6} = \\frac{11\\pi}{3} - \\frac{11\\pi}{3} = 0\\). This means that \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is the same as \\(0\\) radians in the unit circle.\n\nNow, we need to find the sine of \\(0\\) radians:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 0 = 0\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).",
